[2026-03-14T22:48:18.570Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013ziCAFXtxox9wcwFNwoQUT","type":"tool_result","content":"   225→often share a single root cause that can be fixed once to resolve many failures. Running all\n   226→tests first and grouping failures by category is more efficient than fixing tests sequentially.\n   227→\n   228→## Replay Decision Heuristic\n   229→\n   230→**Before opening a Replay recording**, read the full error output and error context snapshot\n   231→(`test-results/*/error-context.md`). If the expected vs received values, selector name, or\n   232→API response clearly identify the root cause, fix directly — do not use Replay speculatively.\n   233→In observed sessions, 44% of Replay usages were speculative (error output would have sufficed).\n   234→\n   235→Use Replay only when error output does not contain the failing assertion's expected/received\n   236→values or when the failure involves visual/layout issues not captured in error snapshots.\n   237→Specifically:\n   238→- **Skip Replay** for data-contamination failures (count mismatches, \"No X found\") — error\n   239→  output is always sufficient.\n   240→- **Skip Replay** for strict-mode violations — the error tells you exactly what matched.\n   241→- **Skip Replay** for invalid assertion API errors (e.g., `toEndWith` not a function).\n   242→- **Use Replay** for CSS/layout issues where visual confirmation is needed.\n   243→- **Use Replay** for complex race conditions where timing is ambiguous from error output.\n   244→- **Use Replay** for backend bugs where error output doesn't explain *why* wrong data exists.\n   245→\n   246→In observed sessions, 100% of Replay uses for data-contamination and strict-mode failures\n   247→were unnecessary — error output was always sufficient. Also skip Replay for seed-data-mismatch\n   248→failures — these are diagnosable by comparing test expectations against seed data or API\n   249→responses without visual debugging.\n   250→\n   251→## Debugging\n   252→\n   253→When tests fail, you MUST follow this process for each distinct failure. Every step is\n   254→mandatory — do NOT skip or reorder steps.\n   255→\n   256→0. **Identify failing tests**: Check `test-results/*/error-context.md` files for readable\n   257→   failure details. Do NOT parse `test-results/results.json` with grep/python3/node — the\n   258→   JSON log reporter strips test names and error details. If error-context files are missing,\n   259→   check `test-results/.last-run.json` for high-level status, then use Replay MCP tools.\n   260→1. Announce `ANALYZING TEST FAILURE: <test name>`.\n   261→2. **Read the debugging guides** in `skills/debugging/` to find the category matching your\n   262→   failure. The guides describe which Replay MCP tools to use first and what to look for:\n   263→   - `skills/debugging/timeouts.md` — Test timeouts and stuck steps\n   264→   - `skills/debugging/race-conditions.md` — Flaky tests, parallel interference\n   265→   - `skills/debugging/component-rendering.md` — Empty DOM, components not mounting\n   266→   - `skills/debugging/network-and-api.md` — API errors, missing/wrong data\n   267→   - `skills/debugging/form-and-input.md` — Form validation, input interactions\n   268→   - `skills/debugging/seed-data.md` — Missing test data, count mismatches\n   269→   - `skills/debugging/README.md` — Quick reference table: symptom → starting tool\n   270→3. Find the uploaded recording ID in the `npm run test` summary line (e.g.,\n   271→   `8 passed, 2 failed (recording: abc123) — see logs/test-run-3.log`). If the summary\n   272→   doesn't include a recording ID, read the log file and look for the\n   273→   `=== REPLAY RECORDINGS METADATA ===` block and `REPLAY UPLOADED: <recordingId>` lines.\n   274→   If the upload did not happen, use `npx replayio list --json` to find recordings and\n   275→   upload them manually: `npx replayio upload <id>`.\n   276→4. Announce `TEST FAILURE UPLOADED: <recordingId>` before doing anything else.\n   277→5. Use Replay MCP tools to analyze the failure, following the tool sequence from the relevant\n   278→   debugging guide. Use as many tools as needed to understand what actually happened before\n   279→   making any changes.\n   280→6. Write a bug writeup to `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` following the investigative template below.\n   281→   You MUST fill in every section before making any code changes.\n   282→7. Only after completing the Replay analysis AND the bug writeup, fix the test and/or app based\n   283→   on what you found.\n   284→8. After fixing the failure, commit your changes and announce:\n   285→   CHANGESET REVISION: <git rev-parse HEAD> FAILING TEST: <test name>\n   286→   This line MUST appear in the log exactly as formatted — report tools search for it.\n   287→\n   288→### Bug Writeup Template\n   289→\n   290→For each test failure, create `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` with exactly this structure:\n   291→\n   292→```\n   293→# Bug: <test name>\n   294→\n   295→## Step 1: Evidence\n   296→\n   297→Evidence the app is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong API\n   298→responses, incorrect state, missing data, UI rendering issues, etc. Write \"None found\" if\n   299→no evidence.>\n   300→\n   301→Evidence the test is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong\n   302→selectors, bad assertions, race conditions, incorrect test data expectations, etc. Write\n   303→\"None found\" if no evidence.>\n   304→\n   305→## Step 2: Determination\n   306→\n   307→Which is broken: <APP or TEST>\n   308→\n   309→## Step 3: Root Cause\n   310→\n   311→<Completely explain the cause of the problem in the app or test. Include the specific file(s),\n   312→function(s), and line(s) involved. Explain WHY the code is wrong, not just WHAT is wrong.>\n   313→```\n   314→\n   315→Do NOT skip Replay analysis and jump straight to reading error messages or guessing at fixes.\n   316→Do NOT skip the bug writeup and jump straight to fixing code.\n   317→Do NOT stop or cancel a recording upload because it is \"taking a while\" — wait for it to complete.\n   318→The Replay recording contains the actual runtime state — use it.\n   319→\n   320→When testing the app after deployment, use the Replay browser to record the app and debug any problems.\n   321→\n   322→## Test Isolation Mandates\n   323→\n   324→These rules are mandatory for all test files. Violations are the most common source of test\n   325→failures (~57% of observed failures come from shared database state).\n   326→\n   327→1. **Mandatory beforeEach database cleanup.** Every spec file that creates, modifies, or\n   328→   deletes records MUST include a `beforeEach` hook that resets the relevant table(s) to seed\n   329→   state via API calls. This single practice prevents the majority of data-contamination\n   330→   failures. The pattern is:\n   331→   ```typescript\n   332→   test.beforeEach(async ({ request }) => {\n   333→     // Delete all non-seed records, then re-seed if needed\n   334→     await request.delete(`${BASE}/api/resource`);\n   335→     await request.post(`${BASE}/api/resource/seed`);\n   336→   });\n   337→   ```\n   338→\n   339→2. **No hardcoded entity counts in serial test files.** Tests MUST use relative assertions\n   340→   (e.g., `toBeGreaterThan(0)`, \"count decreased by 1\") or query initial count before\n   341→   asserting. Never hardcode expected values like \"4 visits\" or \"3 customers\".\n   342→\n   343→3. **Destructive tests go last AND use serial.** Tests that delete all entities (empty state\n   344→   tests) or rename entities MUST be ordered at the end of their describe block AND wrapped\n   345→   in `test.describe.serial`. This must be done during initial test authoring (writeTests),\n   346→   not deferred to checkDirectives. Placing destructive tests earlier corrupts state for\n   347→   subsequent tests — this caused 33% of all failures in one observed session. Any test that\n   348→   modifies or deletes shared data (status changes, renames, deletions) MUST either (a)\n   349→   create its own data in `beforeEach` or (b) restore original state in `afterEach`. This\n   350→   prevents 73% of data-contamination failures caused by destructive-ordering.\n   351→\n   352→4. **Unique entity names per test.** All test data created by tests (customer names,\n   353→   addresses, property names, etc.) MUST include `Date.now()` or `crypto.randomUUID()`\n   354→   suffixes to prevent cross-test contamination. For example, use\n   355→   `Test Customer ${Date.now()}` instead of `Test Customer`. This single practice prevents\n   356→   the majority of data-contamination failures from duplicate name collisions.\n   357→\n   358→5. **TRUNCATE before seeding (Neon branches).** Seed scripts MUST call `truncateAllTables()`\n   359→   before inserting data. Neon branch creation inherits parent data, so insert-only seeding\n   360→   produces duplicates. Always use `truncateAndSeed()`, never `seedDatabase()` alone.\n   361→\n   362→6. **Relative or data-independent assertions.** Tests that verify data after mutations must\n   363→   query current state before the action and assert relative changes, not absolute values.\n   364→\n   365→7. **Status transition tests must reset state.** Tests that change entity status (e.g., RFI\n   366→   status, submittal status) MUST include `beforeEach` API calls to reset the entity back to\n   367→   its initial state. Without this, status transition tests leave entities in terminal states\n   368→   that break subsequent tests. In one session, this pattern caused 14 affected tests across\n   369→   RFI and submittal status spec files.\n   370→\n   371→8. **Distinct test data records per test.** Each test must operate on its own database\n   372→   record (vendor, PO, delivery, etc.) rather than sharing records across tests. Every spec\n   373→   file should create its own test data via API calls in `beforeEach`/`beforeAll` rather\n   374→   than relying on seed data. This is the single highest-impact isolation improvement —\n   375→   57.8% of observed failures came from data contamination when tests shared seed data.\n   376→   In one session, this single change would have prevented 10+ data-contamination failures\n   377→   across receive-delivery, vendor-detail, and PO-related spec files.\n   378→\n   379→   **State reset for destructive operations**: Every `describe` block containing tests that\n   380→   perform destructive operations (delete, modify, toggle) MUST include a `beforeEach` or\n   381→   `beforeAll` hook that resets state to a known baseline. Without this, earlier tests that\n   382→   delete or modify entities corrupt state for subsequent tests. This single practice would\n   383→   have prevented 4 data-contamination clusters (22 affected tests) in one observed session.\n   384→\n   385→9. **No hardcoded seed data UUIDs.** Always discover entity IDs via API by name rather\n   386→   than assuming seed UUIDs exist. Seed record UUIDs may be deleted by earlier tests via\n   387→   cascade, causing failures in later tests that reference them.\n   388→\n   389→10. **Pre-test state verification in serial suites.** Each test in a `test.describe.serial`\n   390→   block should verify its preconditions rather than assuming state from prior tests. For\n   391→   example, check that the expected number of rows exists before performing add/delete\n   392→   operations, rather than relying on a previous test's side effects.\n   393→\n   394→11. **Password-change tests must restore original credentials.** Any spec file that\n   395→    changes a user's password MUST restore the original password in `afterAll` or use a\n   396→    dedicated test user account. Password mutations cascade into 18+ failures across other\n   397→    spec files (e.g., personal-info, messages) that rely on the original credentials for\n   398→    API authentication and `beforeEach` cleanup. This is the highest-blast-radius\n   399→    data-contamination pattern observed — a single password change can break 3+ spec files.\n   400→\n   401→12. **Navigate before `page.evaluate(fetch(...))`**. Tests that use `page.evaluate(fetch(...))`\n   402→   for API-driven setup in `beforeEach` or `beforeAll` MUST call `page.goto()` first to\n   403→   navigate to the app. `fetch()` with relative URLs fails at `about:blank` because there\n   404→   is no base URL to resolve against. Always ensure the page has navigated before making\n   405→   fetch calls via `page.evaluate`.\n   406→\n   407→12. **Weekend-safe seed data.** Seed data must include entries for the current day\n   408→   regardless of day-of-week. Use relative date calculations (e.g., `new Date()`) rather\n   409→   than hardcoded weekday dates. Tests that rely on \"today's appointments\" or similar\n   410→   day-specific queries will fail on weekends if seed data only contains weekday entries.\n   411→\n   412→13. **Use click-based interaction for custom dropdowns.** When the UI uses custom dropdown\n   413→    components (non-native `<select>`), tests must use click-based interaction patterns\n   414→    (`click trigger → click option`), not `page.selectOption()`. Assertions should use\n   415→    `getAttribute('data-value')` instead of `toHaveValue()`.\n   416→\n   417→14. **Validate `data-testid` prefix selectors.** When using `[data-testid^=\"prefix-\"]`\n   418→   selectors, verify that container/wrapper elements don't also match the prefix. A selector\n   419→   like `[data-testid^=\"route-stop-\"]` will match both list items and the container if\n   420→   named `route-stop-list`. Use `:not()` exclusions or more specific selectors to avoid\n   421→   overcounting.\n   422→\n   423→14. **Mandatory beforeEach cleanup in ALL spec files that modify data.** Every spec file\n   424→    that creates, updates, or deletes records MUST include a `beforeEach` hook that resets"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2f4e5d6b-4fb9-41f9-a901-1702b878282d","uuid":"6262d969-0c85-44d9-93a2-c140bfbbff3b","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/repo/skills/tasks/build/testing.md","content":"often share a single root cause that can be fixed once to resolve many failures. Running all\ntests first and grouping failures by category is more efficient than fixing tests sequentially.\n\n## Replay Decision Heuristic\n\n**Before opening a Replay recording**, read the full error output and error context snapshot\n(`test-results/*/error-context.md`). If the expected vs received values, selector name, or\nAPI response clearly identify the root cause, fix directly — do not use Replay speculatively.\nIn observed sessions, 44% of Replay usages were speculative (error output would have sufficed).\n\nUse Replay only when error output does not contain the failing assertion's expected/received\nvalues or when the failure involves visual/layout issues not captured in error snapshots.\nSpecifically:\n- **Skip Replay** for data-contamination failures (count mismatches, \"No X found\") — error\n  output is always sufficient.\n- **Skip Replay** for strict-mode violations — the error tells you exactly what matched.\n- **Skip Replay** for invalid assertion API errors (e.g., `toEndWith` not a function).\n- **Use Replay** for CSS/layout issues where visual confirmation is needed.\n- **Use Replay** for complex race conditions where timing is ambiguous from error output.\n- **Use Replay** for backend bugs where error output doesn't explain *why* wrong data exists.\n\nIn observed sessions, 100% of Replay uses for data-contamination and strict-mode failures\nwere unnecessary — error output was always sufficient. Also skip Replay for seed-data-mismatch\nfailures — these are diagnosable by comparing test expectations against seed data or API\nresponses without visual debugging.\n\n## Debugging\n\nWhen tests fail, you MUST follow this process for each distinct failure. Every step is\nmandatory — do NOT skip or reorder steps.\n\n0. **Identify failing tests**: Check `test-results/*/error-context.md` files for readable\n   failure details. Do NOT parse `test-results/results.json` with grep/python3/node — the\n   JSON log reporter strips test names and error details. If error-context files are missing,\n   check `test-results/.last-run.json` for high-level status, then use Replay MCP tools.\n1. Announce `ANALYZING TEST FAILURE: <test name>`.\n2. **Read the debugging guides** in `skills/debugging/` to find the category matching your\n   failure. The guides describe which Replay MCP tools to use first and what to look for:\n   - `skills/debugging/timeouts.md` — Test timeouts and stuck steps\n   - `skills/debugging/race-conditions.md` — Flaky tests, parallel interference\n   - `skills/debugging/component-rendering.md` — Empty DOM, components not mounting\n   - `skills/debugging/network-and-api.md` — API errors, missing/wrong data\n   - `skills/debugging/form-and-input.md` — Form validation, input interactions\n   - `skills/debugging/seed-data.md` — Missing test data, count mismatches\n   - `skills/debugging/README.md` — Quick reference table: symptom → starting tool\n3. Find the uploaded recording ID in the `npm run test` summary line (e.g.,\n   `8 passed, 2 failed (recording: abc123) — see logs/test-run-3.log`). If the summary\n   doesn't include a recording ID, read the log file and look for the\n   `=== REPLAY RECORDINGS METADATA ===` block and `REPLAY UPLOADED: <recordingId>` lines.\n   If the upload did not happen, use `npx replayio list --json` to find recordings and\n   upload them manually: `npx replayio upload <id>`.\n4. Announce `TEST FAILURE UPLOADED: <recordingId>` before doing anything else.\n5. Use Replay MCP tools to analyze the failure, following the tool sequence from the relevant\n   debugging guide. Use as many tools as needed to understand what actually happened before\n   making any changes.\n6. Write a bug writeup to `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` following the investigative template below.\n   You MUST fill in every section before making any code changes.\n7. Only after completing the Replay analysis AND the bug writeup, fix the test and/or app based\n   on what you found.\n8. After fixing the failure, commit your changes and announce:\n   CHANGESET REVISION: <git rev-parse HEAD> FAILING TEST: <test name>\n   This line MUST appear in the log exactly as formatted — report tools search for it.\n\n### Bug Writeup Template\n\nFor each test failure, create `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` with exactly this structure:\n\n```\n# Bug: <test name>\n\n## Step 1: Evidence\n\nEvidence the app is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong API\nresponses, incorrect state, missing data, UI rendering issues, etc. Write \"None found\" if\nno evidence.>\n\nEvidence the test is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong\nselectors, bad assertions, race conditions, incorrect test data expectations, etc. Write\n\"None found\" if no evidence.>\n\n## Step 2: Determination\n\nWhich is broken: <APP or TEST>\n\n## Step 3: Root Cause\n\n<Completely explain the cause of the problem in the app or test. Include the specific file(s),\nfunction(s), and line(s) involved. Explain WHY the code is wrong, not just WHAT is wrong.>\n```\n\nDo NOT skip Replay analysis and jump straight to reading error messages or guessing at fixes.\nDo NOT skip the bug writeup and jump straight to fixing code.\nDo NOT stop or cancel a recording upload because it is \"taking a while\" — wait for it to complete.\nThe Replay recording contains the actual runtime state — use it.\n\nWhen testing the app after deployment, use the Replay browser to record the app and debug any problems.\n\n## Test Isolation Mandates\n\nThese rules are mandatory for all test files. Violations are the most common source of test\nfailures (~57% of observed failures come from shared database state).\n\n1. **Mandatory beforeEach database cleanup.** Every spec file that creates, modifies, or\n   deletes records MUST include a `beforeEach` hook that resets the relevant table(s) to seed\n   state via API calls. This single practice prevents the majority of data-contamination\n   failures. The pattern is:\n   ```typescript\n   test.beforeEach(async ({ request }) => {\n     // Delete all non-seed records, then re-seed if needed\n     await request.delete(`${BASE}/api/resource`);\n     await request.post(`${BASE}/api/resource/seed`);\n   });\n   ```\n\n2. **No hardcoded entity counts in serial test files.** Tests MUST use relative assertions\n   (e.g., `toBeGreaterThan(0)`, \"count decreased by 1\") or query initial count before\n   asserting. Never hardcode expected values like \"4 visits\" or \"3 customers\".\n\n3. **Destructive tests go last AND use serial.** Tests that delete all entities (empty state\n   tests) or rename entities MUST be ordered at the end of their describe block AND wrapped\n   in `test.describe.serial`. This must be done during initial test authoring (writeTests),\n   not deferred to checkDirectives. Placing destructive tests earlier corrupts state for\n   subsequent tests — this caused 33% of all failures in one observed session. Any test that\n   modifies or deletes shared data (status changes, renames, deletions) MUST either (a)\n   create its own data in `beforeEach` or (b) restore original state in `afterEach`. This\n   prevents 73% of data-contamination failures caused by destructive-ordering.\n\n4. **Unique entity names per test.** All test data created by tests (customer names,\n   addresses, property names, etc.) MUST include `Date.now()` or `crypto.randomUUID()`\n   suffixes to prevent cross-test contamination. For example, use\n   `Test Customer ${Date.now()}` instead of `Test Customer`. This single practice prevents\n   the majority of data-contamination failures from duplicate name collisions.\n\n5. **TRUNCATE before seeding (Neon branches).** Seed scripts MUST call `truncateAllTables()`\n   before inserting data. Neon branch creation inherits parent data, so insert-only seeding\n   produces duplicates. Always use `truncateAndSeed()`, never `seedDatabase()` alone.\n\n6. **Relative or data-independent assertions.** Tests that verify data after mutations must\n   query current state before the action and assert relative changes, not absolute values.\n\n7. **Status transition tests must reset state.** Tests that change entity status (e.g., RFI\n   status, submittal status) MUST include `beforeEach` API calls to reset the entity back to\n   its initial state. Without this, status transition tests leave entities in terminal states\n   that break subsequent tests. In one session, this pattern caused 14 affected tests across\n   RFI and submittal status spec files.\n\n8. **Distinct test data records per test.** Each test must operate on its own database\n   record (vendor, PO, delivery, etc.) rather than sharing records across tests. Every spec\n   file should create its own test data via API calls in `beforeEach`/`beforeAll` rather\n   than relying on seed data. This is the single highest-impact isolation improvement —\n   57.8% of observed failures came from data contamination when tests shared seed data.\n   In one session, this single change would have prevented 10+ data-contamination failures\n   across receive-delivery, vendor-detail, and PO-related spec files.\n\n   **State reset for destructive operations**: Every `describe` block containing tests that\n   perform destructive operations (delete, modify, toggle) MUST include a `beforeEach` or\n   `beforeAll` hook that resets state to a known baseline. Without this, earlier tests that\n   delete or modify entities corrupt state for subsequent tests. This single practice would\n   have prevented 4 data-contamination clusters (22 affected tests) in one observed session.\n\n9. **No hardcoded seed data UUIDs.** Always discover entity IDs via API by name rather\n   than assuming seed UUIDs exist. Seed record UUIDs may be deleted by earlier tests via\n   cascade, causing failures in later tests that reference them.\n\n10. **Pre-test state verification in serial suites.** Each test in a `test.describe.serial`\n   block should verify its preconditions rather than assuming state from prior tests. For\n   example, check that the expected number of rows exists before performing add/delete\n   operations, rather than relying on a previous test's side effects.\n\n11. **Password-change tests must restore original credentials.** Any spec file that\n    changes a user's password MUST restore the original password in `afterAll` or use a\n    dedicated test user account. Password mutations cascade into 18+ failures across other\n    spec files (e.g., personal-info, messages) that rely on the original credentials for\n    API authentication and `beforeEach` cleanup. This is the highest-blast-radius\n    data-contamination pattern observed — a single password change can break 3+ spec files.\n\n12. **Navigate before `page.evaluate(fetch(...))`**. Tests that use `page.evaluate(fetch(...))`\n   for API-driven setup in `beforeEach` or `beforeAll` MUST call `page.goto()` first to\n   navigate to the app. `fetch()` with relative URLs fails at `about:blank` because there\n   is no base URL to resolve against. Always ensure the page has navigated before making\n   fetch calls via `page.evaluate`.\n\n12. **Weekend-safe seed data.** Seed data must include entries for the current day\n   regardless of day-of-week. Use relative date calculations (e.g., `new Date()`) rather\n   than hardcoded weekday dates. Tests that rely on \"today's appointments\" or similar\n   day-specific queries will fail on weekends if seed data only contains weekday entries.\n\n13. **Use click-based interaction for custom dropdowns.** When the UI uses custom dropdown\n    components (non-native `<select>`), tests must use click-based interaction patterns\n    (`click trigger → click option`), not `page.selectOption()`. Assertions should use\n    `getAttribute('data-value')` instead of `toHaveValue()`.\n\n14. **Validate `data-testid` prefix selectors.** When using `[data-testid^=\"prefix-\"]`\n   selectors, verify that container/wrapper elements don't also match the prefix. A selector\n   like `[data-testid^=\"route-stop-\"]` will match both list items and the container if\n   named `route-stop-list`. Use `:not()` exclusions or more specific selectors to avoid\n   overcounting.\n\n14. **Mandatory beforeEach cleanup in ALL spec files that modify data.** Every spec file\n    that creates, updates, or deletes records MUST include a `beforeEach` hook that resets","numLines":200,"startLine":225,"totalLines":924}}}
